Z||000007||Raydar||01-09-2001||06:03 PM||||Okay, did a bit of poking around. The "Shortstar" is, pretty much, 3/4 of a Northstar. It's 3.5 liters and 215 H.P. DOHC.<BR>I don't know of anyone who has done the swap, though. The 4.9 V-8 (pre-Northstar Caddy) is becoming an increasingly popular swap, and is rumored to be fairly easy to do. Both should bolt to the fiero trans with a minimum of fiddling. The 4.3 would be almost as difficult as a small-block Chevy V-8.   ||||reg||1
Z||000008||jerpro||01-09-2001||06:16 PM||||so basically go for the shortstar before the 4.3 because it will be easier to bolt on right?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||loafer87gt||01-09-2001||06:33 PM||||I'm not too sure how difficult a 3.5 SS swap would be. Does anyone know if the Getrag tranny mates to the engine? I think if you are looking for a direct drop in swap, a 3.4 pushrod would be the easiest swap. Also, if your running an 88 Fiero, your cradle should accept a 3.4 DOHC engine, except tat it will take some work getting all the electronics working. If your interested in the 3.4 DOHC swap, do a search for Terry K on the forum, he completed one of these swaps.||||reg||1
Z||000010||jerpro||01-09-2001||06:50 PM||||here's some homework...<BR>is the 3.4 available for the auto trans? <BR>also, will it work on an 84?<BR>There are severaly fiero fanatics that sell kits for engine swaps, I need to know which one would be the most cost efficient reliable and most complete. (a video would be helpfull) <BR>thanks.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||lou_dias||01-09-2001||08:40 PM||||There was a 4.1 v6 90 degree in the 80's and early 90's...<BR>It goes like this:<BR>60degree:  2.8, 3.1, 3.4<BR>90degree:  3.8, 4.1, 4.3<P>by the way, 3.4's aren't quite 3.4's<BR>a bore of 3.64 and stroke of 3.31 is a 3.4L engine, however the bore of the 3.4's is less than that.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Black88GT||01-09-2001||09:14 PM||||lou, you forgot the 90 degree 3.3L buick engine.  <P>There were many rumors on what Pontiac was planning on putting in the '89 Fiero, while its nice to think something better then a 2.8, Motor Trend says it was going to be a 3.1.<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD||||reg||1
Z||000013||lou_dias||01-10-2001||09:23 AM||||Oh yeah, the under bored and understroked 3.8 = 3.3...<P>I would have to agree with MT on the 3.1 because I don't think they had the stronger factory 3.4 block ready for production just yet.  GM plays it safe.  Besides, that would have been the power upgrade path over a turbo setup which costs more and requires more maintainence.  That and an Aluminum cradle and frame would have really made our pride and joys fly...||||reg||1
Z||000014||jerpro||01-10-2001||11:10 AM||||then basically... <BR>WHat is the best engine- cost effective<BR>I can put in my car?<BR>Also an easy conversion.... It's my first one!<BR><p>[This message has been edited by jerpro (edited 01-10-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000015||lou_dias||01-10-2001||12:38 PM||||Cost effective with good performance is a 3.4 pushrod engine, not the Twin Dual Overhead cam 3.4L engine that predates the Northstar.  Too much rewiring with that one.<BR>With the 3.4 pushrod engine, you just drill starter holes on the other side and put on all the other Fiero stuff (heads, intake, water pump and cover, etc...).||||reg||1
Z||000016||MooCow||01-10-2001||03:16 PM||||Ed Parks does the 3.4 pushrod swap.  I am getting an 87 GT this weekend.  I am driving down to go pick it up.  Look at his web site it tells what has to be done. According to him it is pretty simple to do.  Also I think Twin Lake Fiero's does the 3.4 pushrod swap as well and they are a lot closer.  The site for Ed Parks is: <A HREF="http://www.angelfire.com/on/fierofactory/start.html#clutch" TARGET=_blank>http://www.angelfire.com/on/fierofactory/start.html#clutch</A> <P>                            Thanks Ken||||reg||1
